AB  - The element iron is essential for life but its role in the ocean has long been ignored. Only very recently, has iron been recognised as one of the most important nutrients for phytoplankton. A strong link between iron in the ocean and climate has also been discovered because growth of the phytoplankton, and, consequently, carbon dioxide uptake by surface water, is limited by low iron concentrations in large parts of the ocean. Studies of iron in seawater are very complex, however, significant progress has been made during the last decade. Our understanding of iron chemistry in seawater is much improved. The mechanisms used by phytoplankton to acquire this scarce and precious chemical element are also better known. Pioneering work on small scale artificial iron fertilisation of the ocean has provided much evidence regarding the important role of iron in the ocean. However, there is a serious risk of private companies carrying out large scale fertilisation of the oceans in deliberate attempts at manipulating the climate.
