AB  - Many studies have regarded coastal wetland-derived total alkalinity (TA) as a durable pathway for atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2) removal, mainly owing to the formation of bicarbonate during the anaerobic mineralization of biogenic organic matter in wetlands and the subsequent lateral export as TA from intertidal wetlands to the coastal ocean. However, this perspective may overestimate the contribution of wetland lateral carbon export to coastal atmospheric CO2 removal because these ecosystems commonly also export equal or larger amounts of dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) alongside TA. Using a dataset of TA and DIC exports from global saltmarshes and mangroves, we show that wetland-derived lateral inorganic carbon export lowers ratios or differences between TA and DIC in coastal waters, thus elevating coastal ocean CO2 levels and reducing its net CO2 uptake from the atmosphere by a median of 0.033-0.048 Pg C a-1 (at least 15% of the total net uptake, 1Pg = 1015 g). This reduction is larger than carbon burial in sediments of global saltmarshes and mangroves (0.025 Pg C a-1). Our results clarify the role of wetland lateral carbon exports in coastal CO2 uptake and highlight that ocean alkalinity enhancement strategies must increase TA relative to DIC, not merely add TA, to achieve durable CO2 removal. (c) 2026 Science China Press. Published by Elsevier B.V. and Science China Press. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
