KW  - GROWTH
AB  - Coastal mariculture is rapidly expanding and is crucial for ensuring global food security; yet, its environmental footprint, particularly the related carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions, is attracting increasing attention under climate change. This study examined the seasonal CO2 flux dynamics at the water-air interface and dissolved CO2 levels in three aquaculture zones, i.e., bivalve, seaweed, and fish zones, and a control zone in Xihu Harbor, a semi-enclosed bay in eastern Zhejiang Province, China. All zones functioned as net CO2 sources throughout the year, with mean fluxes varying from 57.71 to 153.68 mu mol m- 2 h-1. The bivalve zone consistently exhibited the greatest fluxes, the fish zone temporarily acted as a CO2 sink during summer owing to nutrient-enhanced phytoplankton photosynthesis, and the seaweed zone showed significantly reduced CO2 fluxes during its growing season but remained a net source annually. The chlorophyll-a content, pH, and temperature of seawater explained 70.5%, 18.5%, and 7.1% of the observed variability, respectively. Partial least squares structural equation modeling further revealed that temperature influenced the CO2 flux primarily via indirect pathways, mediated through its influence on chlorophyll-a. These results indicate that the CO2 flux in Xihu Harbor was primarily regulated by the biological pump driven by phytoplankton photosynthesis, outweighing the direct thermodynamic effects of the physical pump. This study offers novel understandings of the ecological processes governing CO2 dynamics in coastal mariculture ecosystems and supports adaptive management strategies, including integrated fish-bivalve-seaweed culture and the optimization of farming intensity, to mitigate carbon emissions and enhance the function of mariculture in blue carbon accounting.
