AB  - Coastal wetlands play a critical role in carbon sequestration, yet their sustainability is increasingly threatened by land-use change and fragmentation. This study investigates the spatiotemporal dynamics of land use, landscape patterns, and carbon storage in China's Jin-Ji (river-dominated) and Dongying (tide-dominated) coastal regions from 1980 to 2020, employing remote sensing, spatial statistics, and scenario modeling. Results reveal three key mechanisms governing wetland sustainability: legacy effects from subsurface carbon retention, scale-dependent fragmentation that shifts from carbon-stabilizing to destabilizing beyond hydrological thresholds, and policy-path dependency constrai Coastal wetlands play a critical role in carbon sequestration, yet their sustainability is increasingly threatened by land-use change and fragmentation. This study investigates the spatiotemporal dynamics of land use, landscape patterns, and carbon storage in China's Jin-Ji (river-dominated) and Dongying (tide-dominated) coastal regions from 1980 to 2020, employing remote sensing, spatial statistics, and scenario modeling. Results reveal three key mechanisms governing wetland sustainability: legacy effects from subsurface carbon retention, scale-dependent fragmentation that shifts from carbon-stabilizing to destabilizing beyond hydrological thresholds, and policy-path dependency constraining future options. Wetland contributions to regional carbon budgets remained modest (<20%), with soil organic carbon (>95% of stocks) far outweighing biomass. Divergent regional responses emerged: Jin-Ji's wetland loss (59%) coincided with carbon stabilization due to legacy retention, while Dongying's saline soil degradation drove disproportionate carbon losses despite area expansion. Landscape metrics demonstrated wetland carbon storage correlates positively with diversity and edge complexity but negatively with connectivity and patch dominance. Scenario modeling underscored that strong intervention-prioritizing saline marsh restoration in Dongying and swamp connectivity in Jin-Ji-optimizes carbon retention while accommodating development. These findings establish functional metrics integrating soil stability and landscape configuration as essential for deltaic carbon management under the Ecological Civilization framework. ning future options. Wetland contributions to regional carbon budgets remained modest (<20%), with soil organic carbon (>95% of stocks) far outweighing biomass. Divergent regional responses emerged: Jin-Ji's wetland loss (59%) coincided with carbon stabilization due to legacy retention, while Dongying's saline soil degradation drove disproportionate carbon losses despite area expansion. Landscape metrics demonstrated wetland carbon storage correlates positively with diversity and edge complexity but negatively with connectivity and patch dominance. Scenario modeling underscored that strong intervention-prioritizing saline marsh restoration in Dongying and swamp connectivity in Jin-Ji-optimizes carbon retention while accommodating development. These findings establish functional metrics integrating soil stability and landscape configuration as essential for deltaic carbon management under the Ecological Civilization framework.
