TI  - Wave attenuation by flexible suspended aquaculture canopies: Insights from experiments and theoretical analysis
T2  - PHYSICS OF FLUIDS
LA  - English
AB  - Suspended longline aquaculture systems, extensively used for cultivating algae and shellfish in China, contribute significantly to global food supply and blue carbon sequestration. Understanding the hydrodynamic processes within these culture regions is critical for ensuring structural safety, maintaining product quality, and managing ecological impacts. However, the flexible connections and dynamic deformations of longline structures introduce significant complexity to the study of the coupling mechanisms between these structures and waves. This study investigates wave propagation through flexible suspended aquaculture canopies using a combination of physical model experiments and simplified theoretical modeling. Laboratory experiments are conducted for two representative forms of longline aquacultural structures, in which the soft connections of the structure components and mooring systems are reasonably modeled to reproduce the wave-canopy interactions. A simplified theoretical model is developed to predict wave transmission, incorporating a bulk drag coefficient adjusted for shielding effects and an effective canopy height to account for canopy flexibility. The experimental and modeled results reveal that wave transmission through suspended canopies is influenced by multiple interacting parameters. Transmitted wave height decreases with increasing wave steepness, and the impact diminishes at higher steepness levels. Shallower water depths result in lower wave transmission through the canopy. A non-monotonic relationship between relative canopy width ( B/L) and wave transmission is observed, with a local transmission peak around B/L=0.6-0.7. Increased canopy density, vertical placement, stiffness of individual culture units, and the ratio of canopy height-to-water depth all contribute to reduced wave transmission, whereas anchoring angle has a comparatively limited effect. Additionally, the spatial distribution of culture units affects the degree of energy dissipation due to varying shielding effects. Spectral analysis indicates that most wave energy dissipation occurs at the fundamental frequency, with rigid canopies showing greater dissipation at higher harmonics. The proposed theoretical model and the findings provide valuable theoretical support for optimizing the aquacultural structure design and spatial layout in marine ranching.
