AB  - THE BIGGER PICTURE To reach climate change goals, significant carbon dioxide (CO2) removal from the environment is necessary. While CO2 removal from the air is well studied, the oceans also contain significant CO2 since CO2 in the air reacts with ocean water to form a bicarbonate buffer; the concentration of dissolved inorganic carbon in the oceans is >100 times larger than the concentration of CO2 in the air. CO2 can be removed from the ocean with a pH swing, i.e., the addition of acid and base, and in this work, we demonstrate a device for electrochemically generating acid and base at up to 500 mA/cm(2). In our device, no expensive, failure-prone ion-exchange membrane is required, and when simulated ocean water is used as the electrolyte, our device is robust to dication precipitation and fouling up to 200 mA/cm(2). Overall, this system provides a route toward efficiently generating acid and base without ion-exchange membranes and without expensive separation of dications from the ocean water electrolyte.
