TI  - Does the optimization of resource allocation in mariculture effectively promote carbon sequestration and carbon emission reduction? --An analysis and test of dual inverted U-shaped relationships
T2  - OCEAN & COASTAL MANAGEMENT
LA  - English
KW  - Green total factor productivity (GTFP)
KW  - Marine aquaculture carbon sink
KW  - Marine aquaculture carbon emission
KW  - Inverted U-Shaped relationship
AB  - As one of the critical pathways to achieving the 2030 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), marine aquaculture possesses dual attributes of both "carbon source" and "carbon sink" in the carbon cycle. However, as the core driver of green development in marine aquaculture, the mechanism through which Green Total Factor Productivity (GTFP) affects carbon sink and carbon emission has not been systematically revealed. Based on this, this paper constructs a systematic framework. While theoretically reviewing the impacts of technological progress on marine aquaculture output, sea area utilization, and related greenhouse gas emissions, it empirically examines the internal relationships between GTFP and carbon sink, as well as carbon emission in marine aquaculture, using panel data from coastal provinces (regions and municipalities) in China. The findings indicate that: (1) GTFP in marine aquaculture exhibits an inverted U-shaped impact on both carbon sink and carbon emission, showing initial promotion followed by inhibition. In the early stage of GTFP improvement, the high price elasticity of demand for marine aquaculture products induces further price reductions from productivity gains to stimulate output expansion, driving synchronized growth in carbon sink and carbon emission. When GTFP crosses the turning point, product demand becomes saturated and the price elasticity of demand gradually declines. At this stage, the technology spillover effect in aquaculture becomes dominant, leading to decreased carbon emission and forming the descending segment of the inverted U-shaped curve. Specifically for marine aquaculture carbon sink, the optimal GTFP interval is approximately 0.9884-1.0514. When GTFP falls within this interval, carbon sink maintains growth while carbon emission has already entered a declining channel, yielding the optimal net carbon sink effect. These conclusions remain robust after a series of robustness checks. (2) Further analysis reveals that the inverted U-shaped relationship between GTFP and carbon sink is more pronounced in regions with high fishery openness. Meanwhile, in areas with superior seawater environmental quality, the nonlinear impact of GTFP on carbon sink is also more prominent, manifesting as a "delayed turning point" effect where the turning point of carbon sink decline is postponed. (3) Marine environmental regulation and technology promotion intensity can effectively moderate the impact of GTFP on carbon emission, facilitating an earlier arrival at the turning point of carbon emission and achieving "advanced turning point," thereby promoting coordinated development of technological progress and low-carbon transition in marine aquaculture. Future research can further optimize the industrial structure of marine aquaculture and systematically enhance GTFP to provide theoretical foundations and pathway support for the deep integration of blue granary construction and low-carbon development strategies.
