AB  - This paper, aligned with the International Maritime Organization's 2050 decarbonization goals, examines the lifecycle assessment (LCA) of marine fuels - both conventional and alternative - used in oxy-fuel combustion (OFC) technology with onboard CO2 capture. It offers a novel analysis of OFC in the maritime sector, utilizing stored O2 for combustion to eliminate nitrogen oxides (NOx) and capture CO2. The study also explores producing e-fuels (e-diesel and e-methanol) from H2 and captured CO2 to establish a closed-loop carbon cycle. In addition to e-fuels, biodiesel is evaluated as an alternative fuel, with heavy fuel oil (HFO) and liquefied natural gas (LNG) serving as conventional benchmarks. Ammonia is assessed as a non-carbon-based fuel for comparison. Using GREET 2024 for LCA inventory, this research compares CO2 emissions from carbon-based fuels under both conventional combustion and OFC in current and 2050 scenarios. Results show that OFC reduces CO2 HFO emissions from 90,000 mg/MJ to 25,000 mg/MJ. Biodiesel with OFC and CO2 capture achieves net-negative emissions (-37,841 mg/MJ). Ammonia achieves emission reductions over conventional fuels if CO2 is captured in the Steam Methane Reforming (SMR) process. By 2050, with H2 produced through electrolysis, e-fuel emissions are expected to decrease by up to 6,042 mg/MJ, a decrease of 84% compared to traditional combustion. Combining biodiesel and e-fuels with OFC emerges as the optimal solution for achieving net-negative emissions and advancing maritime decarbonization by 2050.
