AB  - Salt marshes sequester and store large amounts of carbon due to high rates of primary productivity and saturated, anaerobic sediment conditions which limit organic matter decomposition. Natural disturbances, sea level rise, and historic human impacts to salt marshes have increased impounded water on marsh surfaces, resulting in vegetation loss and the associated loss of important ecosystem services such as carbon storage. Runnels are a climate adaptation technique designed to restore salt marsh habitat by reestablishing a tidal connection between impounded water and a nearby drainage feature. While runnels can drain impounded water and promote revegetation, the unintended effects of altering redox conditions remain unknown. Draining and drying sediments could increase redox potentials, possibly stimulating aerobic decomposition, with negative implications for long term carbon storage. We measured organic matter decomposition in pannes and runneled areas using buried Spartina alterniflora litterbags and the Tea Bag Index (TBI). We also measured runnel effects on drivers of decomposition, including redox potential, moisture content, and porewater salinity. We found that runneling reduced porewater salinity but did not significantly alter redox potential or moisture content. And thus, rates of carbon decomposition, measured by both standard litterbag and TBI methods, were not universally stimulated. Therefore, runnels were used successfully at two Massachusetts sites to restore important salt marsh habitat by restoring tidal flow, without dramatically affecting sediment characteristics or rates of carbon decomposition.
