TI  - Evaluating High-Resolution Satellite Imagery (PlanetScope, Sentinel-2) for Seagrass Monitoring in Korean Coastal Waters
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE KOREAN EARTH SCIENCE SOCIETY
LA  - Korean
KW  - seagrass
KW  - remote sensing
KW  - high resolution satellite
KW  - PlanetScope
KW  - Sentinel-2 MSI
KW  - DEPTH
AB  - Seagrass ecosystems provide vital functions and services to human societies, including biodiversity enhancement, carbon sequestration, food production, and shoreline protection. However, these invaluable ecosystems have been rapidly declining worldwide due to increasing human pressures, including climate change, pollution, and coastal development. Effective monitoring of seagrass habitat-its spatial extent, distribution, and temporal patch dynamics-is becoming increasingly important for conservation, management, and restoration. In many cases, continuous monitoring with higher temporal resolution is necessary to achieve these goals. Yet, the development and application of high-resolution tools for effective seagrass monitoring remain limited in many regions. High-resolution satellite remote sensing offers a promising solution where continuous, large-scale in situ monitoring is difficult. In this study, we assessed the applicability of PlanetScope and Sentinel-2 MSI optical imagery for seagrass monitoring in the southern coast of South Korea (Geoje). We compared several vegetation indices (e.g., NDVI, NDAVI, and GNDVI) and identified the most effective index for seagrass detection. We then verified the applicability of the detection results against drone imagery. This study provides a foundational step toward establishing a high-resolution satellite-based monitoring system for coastal seagrass and highlights its potential role in advancing future conservation and management of seagrass as both a protected species and a critical marine habitat.
