AB  - Native vegetation plays a crucial role in wetland ecosystems, which in the Yellow River Estuary (YRE) is mainly composed of Phragmites australis (P. australis), Suaeda salsa (S. salsa) and Tamarix chinensis (T. chinensis). However, the rapid invasion of Spartina alterniflora (S. alterniflora) has caused significant damage to native vegetation. In 2020, China launched the S. alterniflora remediation plan in the YRE. Here we investigated the impact of ecological management on native vegetation carbon storage in the YRE by generating a monthly 10-m resolution classification dataset of salt marsh vegetation, derived from multi-source remote sensing monthly composites images for 2019 and 2024. Field investigations and Google Earth Engine were employed to analyze changes in carbon storage across different vegetation communities, and the interannual dynamics from 2019 to 2024 were systematically assessed to evaluate the long-term effects of ecological management. Compared with Support Vector Machine and Gradient Boosting Decision Tree, the Random Forest presents the best performance with an overall accuracy of 95.50%. The results indicate that ecological management has led to significant changes in the distribution of native vegetation in the YRE, particularly in some areas once dominated by S. alterniflora. Carbon storage of P. australis and T. chinensis decreased, whereas that of S. salsa increased, especially along the eastern and northern intertidal zones. These spatial patterns may be associated with interspecific differences in salt tolerance and zonation along the salinity gradient. We suggest that S. salsa exhibits greater salt tolerance and environmental adaptability than P. australis and T. chinensis, appears better adapted to colonize high-salinity zones following the removal of S. alterniflora. The recovery of P. australis likely reflects both hydrological fluctuations and ecological management. Emphasizing the distinct responses and traits of different vegetation types is essential for promoting recovery and maintaining ecosystem stability in estuarine wetlands. As current management practices are maintained, S. salsa is likely to assume a more prominent role in the wetland carbon sequestration.
