AB  - Mangrove ecosystems provide critical coastal protection and carbon sequestration but are increasingly stressed by drought due to climate change. This review synthesizes current knowledge on the prospective role of jasmonic acid (JA) in enhancing drought resilience in mangroves. While JA is well established as a key regulator of biotic stress responses in model plants, emerging evidence from mangrove systems indicates that the JA pathway is conservedly activated under stress by JA. We examine mangrove-specific physiological adaptations to drought and synthesize genomic and proteomic evidence to propose a putative mechanism for JA-mediated drought tolerance. This mechanism is hypothesized to operate through the recognized JA signaling pathway, where drought induced JA biosynthesis leads to the degradation of JASMONATE ZIM DOMAIN (JAZ) repressor proteins. This, in turn, liberates transcription factors like MYC2, which activate downstream stress responsive programs, including the enhancement of antioxidant systems, accumulation of osmolytes, and regulation of cell wall remodeling processes critical for maintaining hydraulic integrity and cellular function under water deficit. The present review highlights the potential of JA-based biotechnological applications for mangrove conservation and restoration. We identify critical research gaps and propose an integrated approach that combines molecular insights with ecological management to safeguard mangrove ecosystems.
