AB  - Direct seawater electrolysis (DSE) that couples CO2 capture with H-2 production could mitigate emissions while reducing freshwater use, but it requires cathodes that are active and durable in chloride-rich, scaling-prone seawater. Here we engineer an oxygen-vacancy-rich amorphous ruthenium oxide on titanium (A-RuOx/Ti) as a robust HER cathode for integrated DSE and CO2 capture. The defect-rich amorphous RuOx framework facilitates proton transport to Ru sites and accelerates water dissociation, enabling low HER overpotentials in both 1.0 M NaCl and natural seawater, while exhibiting excellent antifouling behavior and strong tolerance to chloride-induced corrosion. The cathode sustains continuous operation for 1000 h at 100 mA cm(-2) in 1.0 M NaCl and maintains stable performance for > 500 h at 100 mA cm(-2) under DSE. Beyond H-2 generation, the system demonstrates high CO2 capture performance: for flue gas, it achieves > 90% capture efficiency with similar to 0.9 electron utilization at 150 mA cm(-2) and shows pronounced resistance to SO2 poisoning; for direct air capture, stable operation is maintained for > 240 h. An energy analysis indicates that seawater electrolysis contributes similar to 86% of the total capture energy, highlighting HER enhancement via electrode design as a primary lever to reduce overall energy demand.
