AB  - This study aims to validate the stable carbon isotopic composition (delta C-13) of phytoplankton as a tool for detecting submarine leakages of anthropogenic CO2(g), since it is characterised by delta C-13 values significantly lower than the natural CO2 dissolved in oceans. Three culture experiments were carried out to investigate the changes in delta C-13 of the diatom Thalassiosira rotula during growth in an artificially modified medium (ASW). Three different dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) concentrations were tested to verify if carbon availability affects phytoplankton delta C-13. Simultaneously, at each experiment, T. rotula was cultured under natural DIC isotopic composition (delta C-13(DIC)) and carbonate system conditions. The available DIC pool for diatoms grown in ASW was characterised by delta C-13(DIC) values (-44.2 +/- 0.9 parts per thousand) significantly lower than the typical marine range. Through photosynthetic DIC uptake, microalgae delta C-13 rapidly changed, reaching significantly low values (until -43.4 parts per thousand). Moreover, the different DIC concentrations did not affect the diatom delta C-13, exhibiting the same trend in delta C-13 values in the three ASW experiments. The experiments prove that phytoplankton isotopic composition quickly responds to changes in the delta C-13 of the medium, making this approach a promising and low-impact tool for detecting CO2(g) submarine leakages from CO2(g) deposits.
