AB  - This study investigated the distribution and storage of soil organic carbon (SOC) in polluted and virgin (nonpolluted) areas of mangrove forests (Avicennia marina) at forty separate sites along the southern coastline of Saudi Arabia's Red Sea. Three mathematical formulas-allometric, exponential, and sigmoid-were applied to soil carbon data from 40 soil cores, totaling 400 soil samples. We aimed to develop models that could predict the cumulative SOC stocks and volumetric SOC densities throughout soil profiles in polluted and virgin A. marina ecosystems. The sigmoid formula proved most effective in predicting volumetric SOC density distribution, with average R2 values of 0.9284 and 0.9848 for polluted and virgin sites, respectively. For polluted sites, all three formulas effectively predicted cumulative SOC stocks, with the allometric formula (R2 = 0.9999) exhibiting the best performance. For virgin sites, all formulas were applicable, with the sigmoid formula demonstrating superior accuracy (R2 = 0.9999). Consistent with the established role of plant cycling in carbon sequestration, both mangrove polluted and virgin sites displayed elevated SOC contents in the topsoil, with topsoil concentration factors (TCFs) exceeding 0.10. The study found that pollution caused a substantial loss of SOC in mangrove forests. SOC density dropped by 3.1 kg C/m3 (14 %), and SOC stock decreased by 1.6 kg C/m2 (13.9 %). This study's findings will significantly enhance our understanding of SOC distribution in mangrove ecosystems under pollution stress, enabling us to forecast future carbon reserves for conservation and restoration.
